	I think it does, in fact, refer to the AD&D "Hit Dice".  Which refers
to the number of (8-sided dice in AD&D, random number from 1-8 in nethack, but
apparently not random for dragons-they always seem to have the max).  It is
analagous to a character's level of experience.  In AD&D, the various classes
have different "to-hit" tables, meaning that they need different numbers on a
20-sided die to hit their opponents (ie, to hit AC 10, a 1st level fighter may
need a 10, a 1st level cleric needs an 11, a 1st level thief needs a 12, and a
1st level mage might need a 13 or higher).  A 10 Hit Die monster hits as well
as, if not better than, a 10th level fighter/warrior type.  'Course, I don't
know the specifics of how Hack determines whether a hit is scored or not :>




